00:00:57.360 --> 00:01:02.160
video as well the first myth is that you
should only use chemical exfoliants at

00:01:02.160 --> 00:01:08.100
night this myth is about chemical
exfoliants and in particular it's about

00:01:08.100 --> 00:01:12.119
the alpha hydroxy acid exfoliant
glycolic acid there were some studies

00:01:12.119 --> 00:01:16.049
that found that using glycolic acid
could make your skin photosensitive in

00:01:16.049 --> 00:01:19.890
other words it was more sensitive to the
Sun it was easier to get sunburned

00:01:19.890 --> 00:01:23.970
I think this myth came because a lot of
people saw the results of this study and

00:01:23.970 --> 00:01:27.720
assumed that it was because the glycolic
acid and the skin when sunlight was

00:01:27.720 --> 00:01:31.409
shining on it it caused it to react in a
way that it normally wouldn't sort of

00:01:31.409 --> 00:01:35.850
like how citrus oils can do this but the
structure of glycolic acid means that it

00:01:35.850 --> 00:01:39.090
can't interact with the Sun very
strongly and if you look at what

00:01:39.090 --> 00:01:43.530
actually happened in the study it's
pretty clear that this is a myth in the

00:01:43.530 --> 00:01:48.299
study 10% glycolic acid and 2% salicylic
acid was applied to volunteers' skin for

00:01:48.299 --> 00:01:52.680
three and a half weeks then the next day
after the three and a half weeks they

00:01:52.680 --> 00:01:57.180
tested the skin with UV and they found
that the area with 10% glycolic acid was

00:01:57.180 --> 00:02:01.200
more sensitive so in the study there
wasn't actually fresh glycolic acid on

00:02:01.200 --> 00:02:05.700
the skin when it was tested with UV that
means that it's more likely that the

00:02:05.700 --> 00:02:09.690
glycolic acid caused the skin to change
in a way that made it more susceptible to

00:02:09.690 --> 00:02:13.079
UV damage so for example the cells are
flatter and so light gets through more

00:02:13.079 --> 00:02:17.219
easily or there might be less pigment an
interesting part of the study is that

00:02:17.219 --> 00:02:21.060
the area that was treated with salicylic
acid didn't actually get more

00:02:21.060 --> 00:02:25.230
photosensitive and in studies where
salicylic acid was freshly applied on

00:02:25.230 --> 00:02:29.459
the skin and then treated with UV they
actually found that salicylic acid was

00:02:29.459 --> 00:02:33.299
protective you can see that from the
structure of salicylic acid it can

00:02:33.299 --> 00:02:38.340
actually absorb UV salicylic acid also
has anti-inflammatory properties which

00:02:38.340 --> 00:02:42.959
could cool down sunburn what you should
be doing instead just use chemical

00:02:42.959 --> 00:02:47.280
exfoliants wherever they fit into your
routine if you're using glycolic acid or

00:02:47.280 --> 00:02:51.659
any other alpha hydroxy acids make sure
you wear sunscreen as well you should be

00:02:51.659 --> 00:02:54.989
wearing sunscreen anyway because it's
really good for your skin if you want to

00:02:54.989 --> 00:02:58.709
find out more about how to exfoliate
then I have a free exfoliation guide

00:02:58.709 --> 00:03:03.659
that you can download I've got the link
in the description myth number two the

00:03:03.659 --> 00:03:07.019
difference between physical and chemical
sunscreens is that physical sunscreens

00:03:07.019 --> 00:03:11.340
scatter and reflect UV light while
chemical sunscreens absorb UV light and

00:03:11.340 --> 00:03:17.159
convert it to heat I see this myth around
a lot from dermatologists and

00:03:17.159 --> 00:03:20.190
peer-reviewed articles and other sources
that you would normally expect to be

00:03:20.190 --> 00:03:24.449
quite reliable in reality the physical
sunscreens which usually refers to zinc

00:03:24.449 --> 00:03:28.680
oxide and titanium dioxide
they only scatter and reflect about 5%

00:03:28.680 --> 00:03:33.599
of the sun's UV the other 95% is
absorbed and converted to heat

00:03:33.599 --> 00:03:38.430
just like with chemical sunscreens this
might seem like a nitpicky technical

00:03:38.430 --> 00:03:43.049
detail that we shouldn't really worry
too much about but the problem is it

00:03:43.049 --> 00:03:47.459
does lead to people picking physical
sunscreens for reasons that aren't

00:03:47.459 --> 00:03:50.909
actually true for example some people
think that this is the reason why

00:03:50.909 --> 00:03:54.959
chemical sunscreens feel hotter on their
face or that the heat produced by

00:03:54.959 --> 00:03:58.949
chemical sunscreens can make melasma
worse when in reality there's only

00:03:58.949 --> 00:04:03.359
really a five percent difference so
because of this myth some people turn to

00:04:03.359 --> 00:04:06.930
physical sunscreens and then they run
into the biggest drawbacks of these

00:04:06.930 --> 00:04:10.019
which is the fact that they're really
thick they tend to be quite

00:04:10.019 --> 00:04:13.949
uncomfortable and they often give a
white cast which is where when you apply

00:04:13.949 --> 00:04:18.239
it on darker skin not even that dark so
I see this on my skin and then to get

00:04:18.239 --> 00:04:23.099
around these problems people apply less
sunscreen which is definitely bad for

00:04:23.099 --> 00:04:25.470
you you can find out more about how to
pick and use

00:04:25.470 --> 00:04:29.520
sunscreens in my ebook the lab muffin guide 
to basic skincare there is a free sample

00:04:29.520 --> 00:04:33.270
chapter which includes lots of
information on sunscreens I've also done

00:04:33.270 --> 00:04:36.450
lots and lots of videos about sunscreens
I've linked all of this again in the

00:04:36.450 --> 00:04:41.760
description the next myth is that if a
product contains comedogenic ingredients

00:04:41.760 --> 00:04:47.910
it will break you out I've done a video
before on the whole concept of

00:04:47.910 --> 00:04:53.220
comedogenicity but in short the test for
these don't reflect how products are

00:04:53.220 --> 00:04:57.210
used in real life firstly the tests were
done on rabbit ears which are not human

00:04:57.210 --> 00:05:01.500
faces and there was one very important
false positive very early on which was

00:05:01.500 --> 00:05:07.140
petrolatum or petroleum jelly this clogged
rabbit ears but later on they found out

00:05:07.140 --> 00:05:12.540
that on human skin it doesn't clog pores
at all newer tests use human skin but

00:05:12.540 --> 00:05:17.070
they usually use back skin and they
cover the ingredient with a patch which

00:05:17.070 --> 00:05:20.220
is again not how we would use this
product on our skin

00:05:20.220 --> 00:05:24.180
the third big issue is that they usually
use a hundred percent of an ingredient

00:05:24.180 --> 00:05:27.990
in a product we have dilution
ingredients will interact with each

00:05:27.990 --> 00:05:31.890
other and your skin differently if
they've been diluted for example if you

00:05:31.890 --> 00:05:36.030
have pure butter versus butter in a
cake it's a very different experience

00:05:36.030 --> 00:05:39.090
so these comedogenicity ratings
might point you in the right direction

00:05:39.090 --> 00:05:42.270
if you're trying to work out which
product is breaking you out especially

00:05:42.270 --> 00:05:45.690
if it's a product that only has one
ingredient or if it has a really

00:05:45.690 --> 00:05:50.100
comedogenic ingredient as one of the top
ingredients even then different people

00:05:50.100 --> 00:05:53.970
will react differently to the same
product instead what you should do is

00:05:53.970 --> 00:05:57.750
keep track of products that have broken
you out and see if you can work out

00:05:57.750 --> 00:06:01.290
which ingredients are common between
them definitely don't throw out products

00:06:01.290 --> 00:06:04.979
that have worked for you just based on
these ratings you should also add one

00:06:04.979 --> 00:06:08.340
product at a time to your routine so
that you can easily work out which

00:06:08.340 --> 00:06:13.200
product is causing you to break out the
next myth is that medical grade skincare

00:06:13.200 --> 00:06:16.669
is better for your skin

00:06:16.819 --> 00:06:21.830
so firstly to clarify medical-grade
refers to products or brands that are

00:06:21.830 --> 00:06:25.969
only sold in dermatology clinics or in
aesthetics clinics they're also

00:06:25.969 --> 00:06:30.409
sometimes called clinical products or
professional-grade products this is very

00:06:30.409 --> 00:06:33.939
different from prescription medications
like tretinoin cream or over-the-counter

00:06:33.939 --> 00:06:37.909
medications like benzoyl peroxide or
sunscreen there are lots of myths about

00:06:37.909 --> 00:06:41.599
why medical-grade skin care products are
supposed to work better for example

00:06:41.599 --> 00:06:45.080
there's a myth that they're regulated
differently from regular cosmetics which

00:06:45.080 --> 00:06:49.189
is not true so-called medical grade
products like cleansers and moisturizers

00:06:49.189 --> 00:06:53.330
are not regulated differently from
normal brands that you would get in a

00:06:53.330 --> 00:06:57.949
supermarket it is just a marketing term
with no legal meaning there's also the

00:06:57.949 --> 00:07:01.129
myth that they automatically contain
higher quality ingredients that they're

00:07:01.129 --> 00:07:05.150
rigorously tested that they have
clinical studies to back them up again

00:07:05.150 --> 00:07:09.139
this might be true for some brands but
not necessarily just from the term

00:07:09.139 --> 00:07:13.219
medical grade it really depends on what
that specific brand wants to do with

00:07:13.219 --> 00:07:17.990
their development process even though
the term implies that there's all this

00:07:17.990 --> 00:07:23.029
rigorous testing a brand can simply just
slap the term medical grade on their

00:07:23.029 --> 00:07:27.259
product and sell it only through
particular clinics and without any of

00:07:27.259 --> 00:07:30.800
this testing that would be perfectly
fine there's also the fact that lots of

00:07:30.800 --> 00:07:35.349
drugstore products are rigorously tested
especially with the brands that are more

00:07:35.349 --> 00:07:39.409
dermatological there's also this myth
that medical grade products are allowed

00:07:39.409 --> 00:07:44.089
to penetrate more deeply or contain higher
concentrations of actives than so-called

00:07:44.089 --> 00:07:49.129
over-the-counter or regular drugstore
products and again it's not true they

00:07:49.129 --> 00:07:52.580
aren't regulated differently instead
what you should do is use skincare

00:07:52.580 --> 00:07:56.719
products that work for your skin and
your budget and your preferences if a

00:07:56.719 --> 00:08:00.560
brand has studies to back up their
products that's fantastic look at that

00:08:00.560 --> 00:08:05.270
but don't just look at the word medical
grade and expect that that will work

00:08:05.270 --> 00:08:12.520
better the final myth is that oily skin
doesn't require moisturizer

00:08:12.629 --> 00:08:17.399
I've talked about this myth before in my
video on dry and dehydrated skin oily

00:08:17.399 --> 00:08:21.059
skin can produce lots of oil but that
doesn't necessarily mean that it's good

00:08:21.059 --> 00:08:25.649
at holding on to water so for example my
skin the oil that sits on top of your

00:08:25.649 --> 00:08:30.509
skin the sebum isn't actually that good
at holding on to water or preventing

00:08:30.509 --> 00:08:34.319
trans epidermal water loss moisturizers
are designed to help your skin and

00:08:34.319 --> 00:08:37.860
compensate for lack of oil or lack of
water and there are three main types of

00:08:37.860 --> 00:08:43.019
ingredients in these there are occlusives
which sit on top of your skin and trap

00:08:43.019 --> 00:08:47.250
water in there are emollients which help
to soften your skin and there are

00:08:47.250 --> 00:08:51.120
humectants which hold onto water and
keep it on your skin not all of these

00:08:51.120 --> 00:08:54.750
ingredients are oils often occlusives
and emollients are oils but

00:08:54.750 --> 00:08:59.040
humectants are not oily so if your skin
is oily you shouldn't just avoid

00:08:59.040 --> 00:09:03.480
moisturizers instead what you should do
is check if your skin is dehydration

00:09:03.480 --> 00:09:08.790
prone then pick a moisturizer that
addresses that problem if you want a ton

00:09:08.790 --> 00:09:11.819
of detail about picking moisturizers you
can check out my video on dry and

00:09:11.819 --> 00:09:16.470
dehydrated skin I also have a whole
chapter in my ebook that's all about

00:09:16.470 --> 00:09:21.120
picking moisturizers so did you believe
any of these 5 myths I definitely did

